From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: mmc: sunxi: a failed multi-block read re-initialises the controller before the stop

A data error in the middle of a CMD18 left the HY310's eMMC dead until a
power cut (four times by 15.10.2026). Measured with a diagnostic patch (0311;
it prints what finalize_request() clears and is not part of this series;
15.10.2026), every step of it:

 - the error was a data CRC error after 165 of 256 blocks (int_sum 0x80); the
   FIFO held 6 words and BBCR trailed CBCR by 26 bytes, so the DMA side
   kept up;
 - finalize_request() turned the DMA off and reset the FIFO while the
   card was still sending; the FIFO filled up again (STAS 0x02009109:
   full, 256 words), CBCR stopped, and the controller held the card clock
   (RINT bit 10, host data starvation, by the time the stop gave up);
 - CMD12 was taken (CMDR 0x0000414c, start bit cleared) and never
   completed: RINT 0x00000420 after 1000 ms;
 - the block layer's recovery then sent CMD13 (CMDR 0x0000014d, CARG
   0x00010000), which was still pending 120 s later with RINT 0x20 and the
   FIFO full; VideoPlayer waited in __mmc_send_status, jbd2 behind it.

A command needs the card clock, and the clock stays held as long as the
controller is receiving into a full FIFO. Before the stop goes out, bring
the controller back exactly as runtime resume does each time the eMMC
wakes from its 50 ms autosuspend: pulse the reset line, init_host, then
bus width and clock from ios (which restores NTSR, the delay lines, DDR
and the HS400 strobe settings). The idle controller ignores what the card
still drives, CMD12 takes the card out of its data state, and the core's
recovery (CMD13, retries) runs as designed.

Only CMD18: a single block fits the 1 KiB FIFO, writes do not fill it,
and the CMD53 path has its own recovery. This does not explain or remove
the CRC error itself; it makes one survivable.

Measured with this patch (15.10.2026):

 - three data errors forced into a running dd read (the HS400 strobe delay
   moved for 0.25 s each: a start-bit error, a start-bit plus data CRC
   error, and a data CRC error that left the FIFO full - the state that
   stopped the box) were each caught and the controller re-initialised
   within about 50 ms; no CMD12 failed, dd read on without an error
   (2.6 GB), nothing stayed in flight, the ext4 error counts stayed 0;
 - about 2000 natural data CRC errors under Kodi 4K playback (up to 742 a
   minute) did not stop the box once; in the worst storm one dd read still
   failed with an I/O error after all its retries, so this is a net and
   not a cure - the errors themselves are what 0313 removes.
---
 drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c | 40 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -909,6 +909,44 @@
 	mmc_request_done(host->mmc, mrq);
 }
 
+static void sunxi_mmc_set_bus_width(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
+				   unsigned char width);
+static void sunxi_mmc_set_clk(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host, struct mmc_ios *ios);
+
+/*
+ * A read that failed in the middle leaves the controller unable to send any
+ * further command (measured on the HY310, 15.10.2026): finalize_request()
+ * stops the DMA and resets the FIFO while the card is still sending the rest
+ * of the multi-block read, the FIFO fills up again with nobody to empty it,
+ * the controller holds the card clock (flow control; RINT shows the host
+ * data-starvation bit 10), and a command needs that clock. CMD12 is taken
+ * (CMDR start bit cleared) but never completes, and the CMD13 the block
+ * layer's recovery sends next waits forever - every reader of the eMMC ends
+ * up in D state, and only a power cut helps.
+ *
+ * Out of that state the controller has to stop receiving. Bring it back the
+ * way runtime resume does every time the eMMC wakes up (reset line pulsed,
+ * init_host, bus width and clock with all timing registers from ios) before
+ * the stop goes out: the idle controller ignores the data the card still
+ * drives, the card clock runs, CMD12 takes the card out of its data state,
+ * and the core's recovery (CMD13, retry) can do its work.
+ */
+static void sunxi_mmc_reinit_after_read_error(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host)
+{
+	struct mmc_host *mmc = host->mmc;
+
+	dev_warn_ratelimited(mmc_dev(mmc),
+			     "read error: re-initialising the controller (STAS=0x%08x CBCR=0x%08x BBCR=0x%08x)\n",
+			     mmc_readl(host, REG_STAS), mmc_readl(host, REG_CBCR),
+			     mmc_readl(host, REG_BBCR));
+
+	if (!IS_ERR(host->reset))
+		reset_control_reset(host->reset);
+	sunxi_mmc_init_host(host);
+	sunxi_mmc_set_bus_width(host, mmc->ios.bus_width);
+	sunxi_mmc_set_clk(host, &mmc->ios);
+}
+
 static irqreturn_t sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = dev_id;
@@ -1218,6 +1256,8 @@
 	 * Additionally we have wait states within this function
 	 * so having it in a lock is a very bad idea.
 	 */
+	if (mrq->cmd->opcode == MMC_READ_MULTIPLE_BLOCK)
+		sunxi_mmc_reinit_after_read_error(host);
 	sunxi_mmc_send_manual_stop(host, mrq);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, iflags);
